  "slug": "aging-senescence-a873",
  "summary": "Recent preprints link senescent cells in aging human and mouse tissues to spatial niches and perturbation responses.",
  "synthesis": "The shared thread is that senescent cells in aging tissues are being resolved at cell-type and spatial scale. In human omental visceral adipose, older adults show more senescent cells than obesity alone explains, and senescent stem/progenitor and mesothelial cells participate in multicellular signaling that drives inflammation. In human liver, spatial transcriptomics places senescent SenSpots at portal tracts and fibrotic septa during metabolic liver disease progression. In aged mouse liver, senescent cells occupy inflammatory niches with distinct epigenetic senotypes, and p53 loss increases inflammation and metabolic damage, while MDM2 inhibitor-mediated p53 activation is reported to reverse age-linked changes. What is new is the combination of cell-type resolution, spatial context, and perturbation-linked evidence, although these findings derive from preprints and the perturbation evidence is limited to mouse liver.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-23"
}
